  "description": "One-minute skill: How You Locate Sounds.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "A twig snaps to your left. You turn instantly. Your brain located the sound using microsecond timing differences.",
      "visual": "🌲"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Sound reaches your left ear a fraction of a millisecond before your right ear if it comes from the left.",
      "visual": "👂"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "The superior olivary complex in the brainstem calculates these interaural time differences with extraordinary precision.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Owls have asymmetrical ears. One sits higher than the other, giving them vertical sound localization too.",
      "visual": "🦉"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "The shape of your outer ear (pinna) creates frequency distortions that help you distinguish up from down, front from back.",
      "visual": "📡"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "You have two ears for a reason. The tiny delay between them maps sound in 3D space.",
      "visual": "🗺️"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "How does the brain determine the direction of a sound?",
    "options": [
      "By comparing arrival times between the two ears",
      "By measuring loudness only",
      "By using the visual cortex"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
